course: dictionary
lesson: 1673
title: Choose: pick one, or decide
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Choose is a verb. It means to pick one thing from several, like choose a book from the shelf. It also means to decide to do something: «She chose to walk home.» Its other forms are chooses, choosing and chosen.
explain: Read the words around choose to see which meaning it has. In «Read the whole problem first, then choose», you pick the right operation from the choices you have. In «choose a safe place to play», you pick one place from several and decide it is safe.
explain: Choose can also be about what is right. «Understanding the reason does not make the lie right, but it helps you be fair and helps you choose the truth yourself.» Here choose means you decide for yourself to pick the truth over a lie.
fact: «Choose» means to pick one thing from several.
fact: «Choose» also means to decide to do something: «She chose to walk home.»
fact: The past form is «chose», as in «She chose a red pen». With has or have, use «chosen»: «She has chosen a red pen».
fact: Some words are chosen to include everyone, like «happy holidays» instead of «Merry Christmas».
fact: You can check the meaning by swapping in pick or decide: if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
exercise: In class you may be asked to «choose a partner». What does choose mean here? | Teach a lesson | Lose a game | *Pick one person from several | Ask for help
exercise: «She chose to walk home.» What does chose tell you? | She ran home in the rain | *She decided to walk home | She was told to stay indoors | She forgot her bag at school
exercise: Find and copy the past form of choose from this sentence: «Yesterday Sam chose the blue bike.» | choice | choosing | chooses | *chose
test: «Understanding the reason does not make the lie right, but it helps you choose the truth yourself.» What does choose the truth mean? | Tell a lie to be safe | *Pick the truth yourself | Hear the truth from someone else | Forget the truth
test: Which sentence uses choose to mean decide to do something? | Choose a partner for the game | Choose red or blue paint | *Choose to be kind to your friend | Choose a book from the shelf
exam: Which word is the past form of choose, used with no has or have? | *chose | chooses | chosen | choosing
